**Read:** [Materiais de Construção Mecânica — Zolin](../sources/Materiais-de-Construcao-Mecanica-Zolin.lingua#r_1KS5EGZJ8PBFDART80K21J1309), unit 2; [Materials structure and observation — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Materials-Structure-and-Observation-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_57315283EDNWKYC1PQE23G2AKF), Polymer Basics, complete package; [Polymer Physics — MIT 3.063](../sources/Polymer-Physics-MIT-3-063.lingua#r_5EPM1GX0YZDKN71N771PQCWQ5P), lectures 1–2, 7–9 and 17 progressively.

Describe repeat-unit chemistry, chain length, branching, stereoregularity, crosslinking and the distribution of molecular sizes. A polymer name often leaves much of this unspecified. Strong bonds along a chain coexist with different interactions between chains; their available rearrangements depend on time and temperature.

For chains with counts N_i and molar masses M_i, number-average molar mass is ΣN_iM_i/ΣN_i and mass-average molar mass is ΣN_iM_i²/ΣN_iM_i. Equal numbers of 10 and 30 kg mol⁻¹ chains give 20 and 25 kg mol⁻¹, respectively. Dispersity is then 1.25. These averages describe a distribution, not one typical molecule.

**Work:** Reconstruct the example from molecule counts and invert an alternative mass-fraction description. Compare thermoplastic, crosslinked elastomer and thermoset structures. Explain what chain orientation, crystallinity, solvent or filler changes in the description. Identify a measurement capable of distinguishing two nominally identical polymer grades.

**Return:** [Materials engineering](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
